## Permission Endpoints

### **MANDATORY: Server-Side Permission Endpoints**

**CRITICAL**: All permission checks must be performed server-side via dedicated permission endpoints. The client should NEVER manually check permissions based on roles, user ID, or entity state.

### **Permission Endpoint Pattern**

**Standard Permission Endpoint URL:**
```
GET /api/{entity-name}/{id}/permissions
```

**Controller Implementation:**

```csharp
[HttpGet(APIDictionary.Partner + "/{id}/permissions")]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetPartnerPermissions(int id)
{
    var partner = await manager.PartnerManager.GetPartnerAsync(id);
    if (partner == null)
    {
        return NotFound();
    }

    // Verify user can view the entity
    var authResult = await authorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(User, partner, PartnerActions.View);
    if (!authResult.Succeeded)
    {
        return Forbid();
    }

    // Get permissions for the partner
    var canEdit = await authorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(User, partner, PartnerActions.Edit);
    var canDelete = await authorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(User, partner, PartnerActions.Delete);

    var permissions = new
    {
        canView = true, // If we got here, user can view
        canEdit = canEdit.Succeeded,
        canDelete = canDelete.Succeeded
    };

    return Ok(permissions);
}
```

### **Authorization Handler Pattern**

**1. Define Entity Actions:**

```csharp
// In UNOPS.PAO.ContextPermissions/Handlers/{EntityName}Actions.cs
public static class PartnerActions
{
    public static OperationAuthorizationRequirement View = new OperationAuthorizationRequirement { Name = "View" };
    public static OperationAuthorizationRequirement Edit = new OperationAuthorizationRequirement { Name = "Edit" };
    public static OperationAuthorizationRequirement Delete = new OperationAuthorizationRequirement { Name = "Delete" };
}
```

**2. Create Authorization Handler:**

```csharp
// In UNOPS.PAO.ContextPermissions/Handlers/{EntityName}AuthorizationHandler.cs
public class PartnerAuthorizationHandler :
    AuthorizationHandler<OperationAuthorizationRequirement, PartnerModel>
{
    private readonly IExecutionContext executionContext;
    private readonly IManagerWrapper managerWrapper;

    public PartnerAuthorizationHandler(IExecutionContext executionContext, IManagerWrapper managerWrapper)
    {
        this.executionContext = executionContext;
        this.managerWrapper = managerWrapper;
    }

    protected override async Task HandleRequirementAsync(AuthorizationHandlerContext context,
                                                   OperationAuthorizationRequirement requirement,
                                                   PartnerModel partnerModel)
    {
        var userId = int.Parse(context.User.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value ?? "0");

        if (requirement.Name == PartnerActions.View.Name)
        {
            await HandleViewRequirementAsync(context, requirement, partnerModel, userId);
        }
        else if (requirement.Name == PartnerActions.Edit.Name)
        {
            await HandleEditRequirementAsync(context, requirement, partnerModel, userId);
        }
        else if (requirement.Name == PartnerActions.Delete.Name)
        {
            await HandleDeleteRequirementAsync(context, requirement, partnerModel, userId);
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleViewRequirementAsync(AuthorizationHandlerContext context,
                                       OperationAuthorizationRequirement requirement,
                                       PartnerModel partnerModel,
                                       int userId)
    {
        if (await executionContext.CurrentUserHasPermissionAsync(Permission.CanViewAllPartners.Name))
        {
            context.Succeed(requirement);
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleEditRequirementAsync(AuthorizationHandlerContext context,
                                           OperationAuthorizationRequirement requirement,
                                           PartnerModel partnerModel,
                                           int userId)
    {
        if (await executionContext.CurrentUserHasPermissionAsync(Permission.CanEditPartners.Name))
        {
            context.Succeed(requirement);
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleDeleteRequirementAsync(AuthorizationHandlerContext context,
                                           OperationAuthorizationRequirement requirement,
                                           PartnerModel partnerModel,
                                           int userId)
    {
        if (await executionContext.CurrentUserHasPermissionAsync(Permission.CanDeletePartners.Name))
        {
            context.Succeed(requirement);
        }
    }
}
```

**3. Register Authorization Handler** (See "Authorization Handler Registration" section above)

### **Permission Flags by Entity Type**

Different entities expose different permission flags based on their business rules:

**Common Permission Flags:**
- `canView` - Can view the entity
- `canEdit` - Can modify entity fields
- `canDelete` - Can delete the entity
- `canSubmit` - Can submit for review
- `canApprove` - Can approve the entity
- `canActivate` - Can activate the entity
- `canCancel` - Can cancel the entity

**Example Permission Responses:**

```json
// Partner Permissions
{
  "canView": true,
  "canEdit": true,
  "canDelete": false
}

// Contact Permissions
{
  "canView": true,
  "canEdit": true,
  "canDelete": true
}

// Opportunity Permissions
{
  "canView": true,
  "canEdit": false,
  "canDelete": false,
  "canActivate": true
}
```

### **Permission Integration Checklist**

When adding permissions to an entity:

- [ ] Create entity actions class: `{EntityName}Actions.cs` with `OperationAuthorizationRequirement` properties
- [ ] Create authorization handler: `{EntityName}AuthorizationHandler.cs` inheriting from `AuthorizationHandler<OperationAuthorizationRequirement, {EntityName}Model>`
- [ ] Implement permission check methods for each action (View, Edit, Delete, etc.)
- [ ] **CRITICAL**: Register handler in `AuthorizationContextWrapper` and `IAuthorizationContextWrapper`
- [ ] Add permission endpoint in controller: `[HttpGet(APIDictionary.{EntityName} + "/{id}/permissions")]`
- [ ] Verify user can view entity before returning permissions
- [ ] Check all relevant authorization actions using `authorizationService.AuthorizeAsync()`
- [ ] Return anonymous object with permission flags (`canEdit`, `canDelete`, etc.)
- [ ] Consider entity state (stage, status) when determining permissions
- [ ] Never expose permission logic to client - all checks server-side

